# Make the module follow the kernel, without depending on anything else to notice.
#
# WHY THIS IS A CRON ENTRY AND NOT ONLY A HOOK.
#
# The first version of this work put the follower in rc.syshook.d/update, on the reading that
# OPNsense runs that directory after an update installs its packages. The directory exists and
# OPNsense ships a script in it - and on this appliance nothing calls that level. The callers of
# rc.syshook pass facility, stop, start, monitor, upgrade, import, early and carp. There is no
# `update`. A hook placed there is read by nobody, which is the most expensive kind of wrong: it
# looks like a guarantee and it is a file.
#
# So the guarantee is here instead, where it depends on cron and on nothing else. Every five minutes,
# kernel-follow.sh compares the kernel on disk with the stamp beside the installed module. When they
# agree, which is almost always, it reads two small files and exits - no fetch, no build, nothing
# written. When they differ, a new kernel has been installed and has not been booted yet, and that is
# the window: the old kernel is still running so the WAN is alive, and the new kernel on disk names
# the source commit it was built from. It fetches, builds, installs and stamps for that kernel, and
# the next reboot brings the two up together.
#
# FIVE MINUTES IS THE NUMBER THAT MATTERS, and it is chosen against the length of the window rather
# than against the cost of the check. OPNsense installs a kernel and then asks for a reboot; the
# reboot is the operator's, and it is normally minutes to hours away, not seconds. Five minutes fits
# inside that comfortably and costs two file reads when there is nothing to do.
#
# IT IS NOT THE ONLY THING. The update hook is still installed in case a future OPNsense calls that
# level, the bring-up script still refuses a mismatched module rather than loading one silently, and
# it will fall back to the previous module when that one's stamp is an exact match for the running
# kernel. This entry is the layer that does not need anyone's cooperation.
#
# AND IF IT MISSES THE WINDOW ENTIRELY - an update and an immediate reboot inside one interval - the
# refusal at boot is still what happens, which is the behaviour that was always there. What this
# removes is the case where the window was wide open and nothing was watching it.

*/5 * * * * root /usr/local/opnsense/scripts/octep/kernel-follow.sh > /dev/null 2>&1
